{"uncertainty": 0.2, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause: debounce restarts its stability count only on a rising edge of noisy", "summary": "Failure: the triage repro reproduced in icarus with 1 of 1 checks failed and $finish reached at 175000 [evidence:prj-a26914b30e.triage.e2] [run:run-0002]. VeriTriage gave unknown_failure at 30% confidence, top hypothesis RTL bug; this analysis treats that only as a candidate [evidence:prj-a26914b30e.triage.e1] [run:run-0001]. Defect, from reading the approved RTL [artifact:prj-a26914b30e.design-under-investigation.a1]: the module header says a change on noisy 'in either direction' restarts the count, and that a pulse of STABLE edges or fewer, high or low, never reaches clean. The code resets cnt only when (noisy && !prev). On a falling edge cnt is not reset, and while clean is 1 cnt is already saturated at STABLE-1. Causality, traced by hand from the RTL text with STABLE=4: at the first low edge, noisy != prev, so clean holds and cnt stays at 3. At the second low edge, noisy == prev and cnt == 3, so clean <= 0. A 2-edge low glitch therefore reaches clean, which the header forbids. Rising edges restart correctly, so high glitches are filtered. That asymmetry matches the file name 'rise_only_restart'. Incorrectness: the restart condition must be (noisy != prev), as the header clause requires. Ranked hypotheses: (1) rtl_bug is supported by the code and clause reading above; it would be refuted if the repro passed on this RTL or failed on RTL that restarts on both edges. (2) testbench_bug is rejected as primary, because the new repro below takes its expected values from the header clause and checks only settled values well away from the STABLE boundary. It would be refuted if this repro failed on clean RTL. The triage testbench content was not available to me. (3) infrastructure is rejected because the triage failure was deterministic and reached $finish [evidence:prj-a26914b30e.triage.e2]. A clean rebuild has not been cited, so this is not fully excluded. (4) spec_ambiguity is rejected because the governing clause explicitly fixes both directions, so no reading allows a low glitch through. This would be refuted only if the project spec contradicted the RTL header. Traces to [requirement:req-19189b08df]."}], "files": [{"path": "debounce_rca_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: a short low glitch must not reach clean", "summary": "Self-checking testbench for the approved debounce RTL [artifact:prj-a26914b30e.design-under-investigation.a1], serving [requirement:req-19189b08df]. Expected values come from the header clause ('either direction restarts the count'). Steps: settle noisy high, check clean=1. Apply a 3-edge low glitch and check clean stays 1 for 15 cycles. Settle low, check clean=0. Apply a 3-edge high glitch and check clean stays 0. All checks avoid the STABLE timing boundary. Hand analysis predicts low-glitch checks fail on the rise-only-restart RTL and all checks pass on correct RTL. Prints one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict with the run nonce, then $finish.", "entry": "debounce_rca_repro"}], "tool_runs": [], "claims": ["By hand analysis of the RTL text, a 2- or 3-edge low pulse on noisy drives clean to 0 when clean was 1. This analysis is not backed by a simulation I ran.", "The fix is expected to be changing the restart condition from (noisy && !prev) to (noisy != prev). It has not been simulated.", "The new repro is expected to fail on the approved RTL and pass on a correct debouncer. The platform's simulator.run gate will determine the first part."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Independent review is still required (review_record); I do not approve my own work. The repro drives inputs on negedge and samples clean on negedge, before each new drive, to avoid races. Infrastructure is weakly excluded because no clean-rebuild run is cited; a reviewer may want one."}
